Chapter: DtlsHello
  cites Foreword chapter Tls
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The ClientHello, ServerHello and HelloRetryRequest bodies for DTLS 1.3
 (RFC 8446 section 4.1, which RFC 9147 inherits whole).

 THE HELLORETRYREQUEST IS NOT A MESSAGE TYPE. DTLS 1.2 had a distinct
 HelloVerifyRequest; DTLS 1.3 does not. A HelloRetryRequest is a
 ServerHello -- same message type, same structure -- whose random field
 is a fixed magic value the working group published (RFC 8446 4.1.3). A
 receiver that switches on message type will never see an HRR; it must
 compare the random against the magic. Getting this wrong means the
 cookie exchange silently never happens, and with it the cookie's
 denial-of-service defence.

 No certificate, no CertificateVerify, and therefore NO PEER
 AUTHENTICATION. A handshake built only from these
 bodies is an ANONYMOUS key agreement. It resists a passive eavesdropper
 and it does NOT resist an active man in the middle, who can simply run
 two handshakes and sit between them. Do not describe a connection built
 on this as secure.

Section: Constants

 RFC 8446 section 4.1.3. A ServerHello carrying THIS random is a
 HelloRetryRequest. It is the SHA-256 of the string "HelloRetryRequest",
 chosen so it cannot collide with a real random by accident.

  dtls-hrr-random : List Integer =
    [207, 33, 173, 116, 229, 154, 97, 17, 190, 29, 140, 2, 30, 101, 184, 145,
     194, 162, 17, 22, 122, 187, 140, 94, 7, 158, 9, 226, 200, 168, 51, 156]

  dtls-ext-cookie-type : Integer = 44
  dtls-legacy-version : List Integer = [3, 3]
  dtls-supported-version : List Integer = [3, 4]

 RFC 9147 section 5.3: a DTLS 1.3 hello's legacy_version is 0xFEFD (DTLS 1.2), and DTLS 1.3 is 0xFEFC in
 supported_versions. The dtls- builders without the 13 carry TLS 1.3's 0x0303 and 0x0304 for the TLS endpoint.

  dtls13-legacy-version : List Integer = [254, 253]
  dtls13-version : List Integer = [254, 252]

  dtls13-ext-versions-client : List Integer =
    tls-be16 tls-ext-supported-versions & tls-be16 3 & [2] & dtls13-version

  dtls13-ext-versions-server : List Integer =
    tls-be16 tls-ext-supported-versions & tls-be16 2 & dtls13-version

Section: Cookie Extension

 RFC 8446 section 4.2.2. The cookie is an opaque vector, so it carries
 its own two-byte length inside the extension's two-byte length.

  dtls-ext-cookie : List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ext-cookie (cookie) =
    let inner = tls-be16 (list-length cookie) & cookie
    in tls-be16 dtls-ext-cookie-type & tls-be16 (list-length inner) & inner

 The server's supported_versions is a single version, not a list -- the
 client sends a vector, the server answers with the one it picked. The
 asymmetry is real and a shared encoder gets it wrong.

  dtls-ext-versions-server : List Integer =
    tls-be16 tls-ext-supported-versions & tls-be16 2 & dtls-supported-version

 The server's key_share is a bare KeyShareEntry -- again, no outer vector,
 unlike the client's list of them.

  dtls-ext-key-share-server : List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ext-key-share-server (public-key) =
    let entry = tls-be16 tls-group-x25519 & tls-be16 32 & public-key
    in tls-be16 tls-ext-key-share & tls-be16 (list-length entry) & entry

Section: ClientHello

  dtls-client-hello-body : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-client-hello-body (random) (session-id) (public-key) (cookie) =
    dtls-client-hello-with dtls-legacy-version [] tls-ext-versions random session-id public-key cookie

 RFC 9147 5.3: a DTLS 1.3 ClientHello carries legacy_cookie after legacy_session_id, and a DTLS 1.3 client sends it
 empty (one length byte, 0). The cookie a HelloRetryRequest asks for travels in the cookie extension.

  dtls13-client-hello-body : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls13-client-hello-body (random) (session-id) (public-key) (cookie) =
    dtls-client-hello-with dtls13-legacy-version [0] dtls13-ext-versions-client random session-id public-key cookie

  dtls-client-hello-with : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-client-hello-with (legacy) (legacy-cookie) (versions) (random) (session-id) (public-key) (cookie) =
    let suites = tls-be16 2 & tls-be16 tls-cipher-aes-128-gcm-sha256
    in let exts = dtls-ch-extensions-with versions public-key cookie
    in legacy & random & [list-length session-id] & session-id & legacy-cookie & suites & [1, 0] & tls-be16 (list-length exts) & exts

 SIGNATURE_ALGORITHMS IS MANDATORY. RFC 8446 section 9.2 requires it of
 any client offering certificate authentication, and a conforming server
 MUST abort without it. mosquitto's OpenSSL answers `missing sigalgs
 extension` and closes with no alert, which from the client's seat looks
 exactly like a server that never replied.

  dtls-ch-extensions : List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ch-extensions (public-key) (cookie) = dtls-ch-extensions-with tls-ext-versions public-key cookie

  dtls-ch-extensions-with : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ch-extensions-with (versions) (public-key) (cookie) =
    let base = versions & tls-ext-groups & tls-ext-sig-algs & tls-ext-key-share-client public-key
    in if list-length cookie == 0 then base else base & dtls-ext-cookie cookie

 THE SESSION ID MUST BE ECHOED. RFC 8446 section 4.1.3:
 legacy_session_id_echo is "the contents of the client's
 legacy_session_id field", and a client that finds anything else there
 aborts with illegal_parameter. TLS 1.3 has no sessions -- the field
 exists only so a TLS 1.2 middlebox sees a shape it recognises (appendix
 D.4), which is exactly why real clients put 32 random bytes in it and
 exactly why a server that ignores it fails against all of them.

 The field sits at a fixed offset in both TLS and DTLS: version(2) and
 random(32) precede it, so the length byte is at 34 whatever comes after.

  dtls-ch-session-id : List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ch-session-id (ch-body) =
    if list-length ch-body < 35 then []
    else let n = list-at ch-body 34
    in if 35 + n > list-length ch-body then []
    else dtls-take-at ch-body 35 n

Section: ServerHello and HelloRetryRequest

  dtls-server-hello-body : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-server-hello-body (random) (session-id) (public-key) =
    dtls-server-hello-with dtls-legacy-version dtls-ext-versions-server random session-id public-key

  dtls13-server-hello-body : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls13-server-hello-body (random) (session-id) (public-key) =
    dtls-server-hello-with dtls13-legacy-version dtls13-ext-versions-server random session-id public-key

  dtls-server-hello-with : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-server-hello-with (legacy) (versions) (random) (session-id) (public-key) =
    let exts = versions & dtls-ext-key-share-server public-key
    in legacy & random & [list-length session-id] & session-id & tls-be16 tls-cipher-aes-128-gcm-sha256 & [0] & tls-be16 (list-length exts) & exts

 The HelloRetryRequest: the same structure, the magic random, and a
 cookie instead of a key share. The server has allocated nothing at this
 point and must not -- the cookie is what lets it stay stateless.

  dtls-hrr-body : List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-hrr-body (session-id) (cookie) = dtls-hrr-with dtls-legacy-version dtls-ext-versions-server session-id cookie

  dtls13-hrr-body : List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls13-hrr-body (session-id) (cookie) = dtls-hrr-with dtls13-legacy-version dtls13-ext-versions-server session-id cookie

  dtls-hrr-with : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-hrr-with (legacy) (versions) (session-id) (cookie) =
    let exts = versions & dtls-ext-cookie cookie
    in legacy & dtls-hrr-random & [list-length session-id] & session-id & tls-be16 tls-cipher-aes-128-gcm-sha256 & [0] & tls-be16 (list-length exts) & exts

 The DTLS 1.3 version fields of a hello, as the endpoint checks them on receive: legacy_version 0xFEFD, and 0xFEFC
 in supported_versions (a ServerHello carries the one version; a ClientHello a vector with a 1-byte length).

  dtls13-legacy-ok : List Integer -> Boolean
  dtls13-legacy-ok (body) =
    if list-length body < 2 then False
    else if list-at body 0 /= 254 then False
    else list-at body 1 == 253

  dtls13-sh-version-ok : List Integer -> Boolean
  dtls13-sh-version-ok (sh-body) =
    when dtls-sh-find-ext sh-body tls-ext-supported-versions
      is None -> False
      is Just (v) -> if list-length v /= 2 then False else (if list-at v 0 /= 254 then False else list-at v 1 == 252)

  dtls13-ch-offers-version : List Integer -> Boolean
  dtls13-ch-offers-version (ch-body) =
    when dtls13-ch-find-ext ch-body tls-ext-supported-versions
      is None -> False
      is Just (v) -> if list-length v < 3 then False else dtls13-versions-has v 1 (1 + list-at v 0)

  dtls13-versions-has : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dtls13-versions-has (v) (i) (stop) =
    if i + 1 >= stop then False
    else if i + 1 >= list-length v then False
    else if list-at v i == 254 & list-at v (i + 1) == 252 then True
    else dtls13-versions-has v (i + 2) stop

 RFC 8446 section 4.2.8: a HelloRetryRequest asking for a key share
 carries key_share as the bare selected_group, two bytes, no key.

  dtls-hrr-group-body : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-hrr-group-body (session-id) (group) =
    let exts = dtls-ext-versions-server & tls-be16 tls-ext-key-share & tls-be16 2 & tls-be16 group
    in dtls-legacy-version & dtls-hrr-random & [list-length session-id] & session-id & tls-be16 tls-cipher-aes-128-gcm-sha256 & [0] & tls-be16 (list-length exts) & exts

  dtls-is-hrr : List Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-is-hrr (sh-body) =
    if list-length sh-body < 34 then False
    else dtls-eq-at sh-body 2 dtls-hrr-random 0 32

  dtls-eq-at : List Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-eq-at (a) (ao) (b) (bo) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then True
    else if list-at a (ao) /= list-at b (bo) then False
    else dtls-eq-at a (ao + 1) b (bo + 1) (n - 1)

Section: Parsing the Server's Answer

 Walk the extension list and pull out what we need. The extensions block
 starts after version(2) + random(32) + session_id + cipher_suite(2) +
 compression(1), and every hop is length-prefixed -- so every hop is a
 chance to be lied to. Each one is bounds-checked against the buffer we
 actually have, not against the length the peer claims.

  dtls-sh-ext-offset : List Integer -> Integer
  dtls-sh-ext-offset (b) =
    let sid-len = list-at b 34
    in 34 + 1 + sid-len + 2 + 1 + 2

  dtls-sh-find-ext : List Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-sh-find-ext (b) (want) =
    if list-length b < 40 then None
    else dtls-ext-scan b (dtls-sh-ext-offset b) want

  dtls-ext-scan : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ext-scan (b) (off) (want) =
    if off + 4 > list-length b then None
    else dtls-ext-scan-at b off want

  dtls-ext-scan-at : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ext-scan-at (b) (off) (want) =
    let etype = bit-or (bit-shl (list-at b off) 8) (list-at b (off + 1))
    in let elen = bit-or (bit-shl (list-at b (off + 2)) 8) (list-at b (off + 3))
    in if off + 4 + elen > list-length b then None
    else if etype == want then Just (dtls-take-at b (off + 4) elen)
    else dtls-ext-scan b (off + 4 + elen) want

 The server's key share: inside the extension, a KeyShareEntry of group,
 length, key.

  dtls-sh-key-share : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-sh-key-share (sh-body) =
    when dtls-sh-find-ext sh-body tls-ext-key-share
      is None -> None
      is Just (e) -> if list-length e < 36 then None else Just (dtls-take-at e 4 32)

 The cookie out of a HelloRetryRequest: inside the extension, an opaque
 vector with its own length.

  dtls-hrr-cookie : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-hrr-cookie (sh-body) =
    when dtls-sh-find-ext sh-body dtls-ext-cookie-type
      is None -> None
      is Just (e) -> if list-length e < 2 then None else dtls-cookie-inner e

  dtls-cookie-inner : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-cookie-inner (e) =
    let n = bit-or (bit-shl (list-at e 0) 8) (list-at e 1)
    in if 2 + n > list-length e then None else Just (dtls-take-at e 2 n)

Section: Parsing the Client's Hello

 A ClientHello is NOT a ServerHello with the fields in the same places.
 It carries a cipher_suites vector and a compression_methods vector that
 the server's hello does not, so the extension block sits at a different
 offset.

 Layout: version(2), random(32), session_id(1+n), cipher_suites(2+n),
 compression(1+n), extensions(2+n).

  dtls-ch-ext-offset : List Integer -> Integer
  dtls-ch-ext-offset (b) =
    let n = list-length b
    in if 35 > n then n
    else let sid = list-at b 34
    in let o1 = 35 + sid
    in if o1 + 2 > n then n
    else let cs = bit-or (bit-shl (list-at b o1) 8) (list-at b (o1 + 1))
    in let o2 = o1 + 2 + cs
    in if o2 + 1 > n then n
    else let comp = list-at b o2
    in o2 + 1 + comp + 2

 The DTLS 1.3 ClientHello's offsets: legacy_cookie (a length byte and its bytes) sits between legacy_session_id and
 cipher_suites. dtls13-ch-fields-ok holds what RFC 9147 5.3 and RFC 8446 4.1.2 require of the fixed fields here:
 legacy_cookie empty, legacy_session_id at most 32 bytes, cipher_suites of an even, non-zero length holding
 TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256, and legacy_compression_methods the single byte 0.

  dtls13-ch-ext-offset : List Integer -> Integer
  dtls13-ch-ext-offset (b) =
    let n = list-length b
    in if 36 > n then n
    else let o0 = 35 + list-at b 34
    in if o0 + 1 > n then n
    else let o1 = o0 + 1 + list-at b o0
    in if o1 + 2 > n then n
    else let o2 = o1 + 2 + (list-at b o1 * 256 + list-at b (o1 + 1))
    in if o2 + 1 > n then n
    else o2 + 1 + list-at b o2 + 2

  dtls13-ch-find-ext : List Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls13-ch-find-ext (b) (want) =
    if list-length b < 41 then None
    else dtls-ext-scan b (dtls13-ch-ext-offset b) want

  dtls13-ch-key-share : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls13-ch-key-share (ch-body) =
    when dtls13-ch-find-ext ch-body tls-ext-key-share
      is None -> None
      is Just (e) -> dtls-key-share-x25519 e

  dtls13-ch-cookie : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls13-ch-cookie (ch-body) =
    when dtls13-ch-find-ext ch-body dtls-ext-cookie-type
      is None -> None
      is Just (e) -> if list-length e < 2 then None else dtls-cookie-inner e

  dtls13-ch-fields-ok : List Integer -> Boolean
  dtls13-ch-fields-ok (b) =
    let n = list-length b
    in if 36 > n then False
    else if list-at b 34 > 32 then False
    else let o0 = 35 + list-at b 34
    in if o0 + 1 > n then False
    else if list-at b o0 /= 0 then False
    else let o1 = o0 + 1
    in if o1 + 2 > n then False
    else if list-at b o1 * 256 + list-at b (o1 + 1) == 0 then False
    else if bit-and (list-at b (o1 + 1)) 1 == 1 then False
    else let o2 = o1 + 2 + (list-at b o1 * 256 + list-at b (o1 + 1))
    in if o2 + 2 > n then False
    else if dtls13-suites-has b (o1 + 2) o2 == False then False
    else if list-at b o2 /= 1 then False
    else list-at b (o2 + 1) == 0

  dtls13-suites-has : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dtls13-suites-has (b) (i) (stop) =
    if i + 1 >= stop then False
    else if list-at b i * 256 + list-at b (i + 1) == tls-cipher-aes-128-gcm-sha256 then True
    else dtls13-suites-has b (i + 2) stop

  dtls-ch-find-ext : List Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ch-find-ext (b) (want) =
    if list-length b < 40 then None
    else dtls-ext-scan b (dtls-ch-ext-offset b) want

 The client's key_share is a LIST of KeyShareEntry, unlike the server's
 bare one: a 2-byte list length, then entries of group(2) length(2) key.
 A client may lead with GREASE (RFC 8701) or a group we do not speak, so the
 X25519 entry (group 0x001D = 29, 32-byte key) is found by walking the list.

  dtls-ch-key-share : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ch-key-share (ch-body) =
    when dtls-ch-find-ext ch-body tls-ext-key-share
      is None -> None
      is Just (e) -> dtls-key-share-x25519 e

  dtls-key-share-x25519 : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-key-share-x25519 (e) =
    if list-length e < 2 then None
    else let declared = 2 + list-at e 0 * 256 + list-at e 1
    in dtls-ks-find e 2 (if declared < list-length e then declared else list-length e)

  dtls-ks-find : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ks-find (e) (off) (stop) =
    if off + 4 > stop then None
    else let klen = list-at e (off + 2) * 256 + list-at e (off + 3)
    in if list-at e off * 256 + list-at e (off + 1) == 29 & klen == 32 & off + 36 <= stop then
      Just (dtls-take-at e (off + 4) 32)
    else dtls-ks-find e (off + 4 + klen) stop

 RFC 8446 sections 4.2.7 and 4.2.3: supported_groups and
 signature_algorithms are each a 2-byte list length, then 2-byte ids.

  dtls-ch-offers-group : List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-ch-offers-group (ch-body) (group) = dtls-ch-offers-in ch-body tls-ext-supported-groups group

  dtls-ch-offers-in : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-ch-offers-in (ch-body) (ext) (id) =
    when dtls-ch-find-ext ch-body ext
      is None -> False
      is Just (e) -> dtls-groups-has e 2 id

  dtls-groups-has : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-groups-has (e) (off) (group) =
    if off + 2 > list-length e then False
    else if list-at e off * 256 + list-at e (off + 1) == group then True
    else dtls-groups-has e (off + 2) group

  dtls-ch-cookie : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ch-cookie (ch-body) =
    when dtls-ch-find-ext ch-body dtls-ext-cookie-type
      is None -> None
      is Just (e) -> if list-length e < 2 then None else dtls-cookie-inner e

Section: Helpers

  dtls-take-at : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-take-at (bs) (off) (n) = dtls-take-loop bs off n 0 []

  dtls-take-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-take-loop (bs) (off) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if off + i >= list-length bs then acc
    else dtls-take-loop bs off n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs (off + i)))
